      "inputTokens": 98,
      "outputTokens": 724,
      "costUsd": 0.006565,
      "pronunciationText": "Look at the two graphs. Each day's planting goes up by the same thirty seedlings, so the daily amounts lie on a straight line. a sub n is thirty n plus twenty, a linear function of the day number. That's what linear growth means: the same change in every period. But the running total adds a bigger amount each day, so its graph bends upward. The totals grow faster and faster, even though each day's planting only grows by thirty."
