      "costUsd": 0.0083605,
      "pronunciationText": "Arithmetic models can go down, too. A new machine makes nine hundred parts in its first week, and fifteen fewer parts each week after that. Here d is minus fifteen, so its output in week n is nine hundred and fifteen, minus fifteen n. That reaches zero in week sixty-one. After that, the formula gives negative parts, which can't happen, so the model only works up to there. How many parts does it make before its output reaches zero? That's the first sixty weeks: thirty, times nine hundred plus fifteen, which is twenty-seven thousand, four hundred and fifty."
